{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_056cc09d4874ff9c0169bb19bdb65c8191ac994bd43097d673", "created": 1773869501, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "**HYSTERECTOMY** is a surgical procedure to remove the uterus, which permanently eliminates the ability to carry a pregnancy in the uterus. In many cases—especially in the context of cervical cancer—the cervix is also removed; when the uterus and cervix are removed together, it is termed a **total (complete) hysterectomy**. Depending on the clinical situation, hysterectomy may also include removal of additional tissues such as parts of the vagina and cervical connective/supporting tissues.\n\nIn cervical cancer care, **HYSTERECTOMY** may be considered as a treatment option in selected situations and is commonly used for **early-stage cervical cancer**, with the extent of surgery tailored to the disease and patient factors. Surgical variants include **extrafascial (simple) hysterectomy**, **modified radical hysterectomy**, and **radical hysterectomy**, with selection influenced by factors such as cancer stage, fertility goals, the presence of lymphovascular space invasion (LVSI), and overall risk features. The operation may also involve **lymph node evaluation or removal** as part of staging and treatment planning, and depending on pathology and risk, it can be followed by **adjuvant radiation or chemoradiation**.\n\nThe ovaries may be **removed or preserved** during **HYSTERECTOMY**. If the ovaries are removed, the patient will experience **surgical (premature) menopause**; if they are left in place, ovarian hormonal function may continue.\n\nWhile hysterectomy is a potential treatment for cervical cancer in appropriate cases, it is generally **not performed when the cancer has spread beyond the cervix**, where other approaches—often including lymph node assessment and non-surgical treatments—may